\oscclient creates an OSC client, to send OSC messages.
It is the same as \osc. Its syntax is the following:

\oscclient myosc

The name can't be the name of a LiveLily command.
Once created, the client is used like a command with its
name, and its methods are concatenated with a dot.

The setup method sets the IP and the port to send to:

\myosc.setup 192.168.1.10 8000

If only the port is given, localhost is used:

\myosc.setup 8000

The send method sends a message. The first argument is
the OSC address, which must start with a / and be inside
double quotes, otherwise you will get an error. It can be
followed by any number of arguments:

\myosc.send "/addr" 57 3.14 "hello world"

Integers are sent as integers, decimal numbers as floats
and quoted text as strings. Any unquoted words are joined
together and sent as one string, after the other
arguments.
Commands that return values can be used as arguments:

\myosc.send "/barnum" \barnum

setup and send can't be combined in the same line.
